INT. ICE-FISHING SHANTY - ARCTIC LAKE - NIGHT

A plywood box on a frozen lake. A stove glows red. Two holes in the floor, black water showing, lines trembling. Frost on the walls. The wind outside is a crowd of ghosts.

WALLACE "WALLY" BRODERICK, fifties, big man, gutting a fish at the table. EMMANUEL OKAFOR-NWANKWO, thirties, schoolteacher, huddles by the stove with a mug. Neither says much.

The door bangs. Snow whips in. A figure in a snow-crusted parka stumbles inside, pulls the door shut, and leans against it, gasping.

STRANGER
Thank God. I saw the light.

WALLY
Sit. Warm up.

The Stranger drops onto a bucket, pulls off his hood. A young man, lips blue, eyebrows frosted. His hands shake. Emmanuel passes him the mug without a word. He drinks. His eyes flick to the knife in Wally's hand, then to the fish, then away.

EMMANUEL
What's your name?

STRANGER
Jonas.

EMMANUEL
Where are you headed, Jonas?

JONAS
The north shore. My truck died.

WALLY
There's no road on the north shore.

JONAS
There is a cabin.

WALLY
There's no cabin.

JONAS
Then I was lied to.

He laughs, uneasy. Emmanuel sets a hand on the stove and warms it. Wally slices the fish with a short, sure stroke, and for a while only the knife speaks.

EMMANUEL
He's right, Wally. People get lost.

WALLY
People do.

Jonas finishes the mug and sets it down. He stretches his hands to the fire and looks, for the first time, relaxed. Wally slides a piece of fish across to him on the blade. Jonas takes it, chews, nods thanks.

JONAS
Who's got the line?

WALLY
He does.

Wally nods at Emmanuel. Emmanuel lifts the line. A fish thrashes. He hauls. Jonas leans in to help. They both grip the line. Wally watches them, his knife stilled in his hand.

The fish comes up, a fat lake trout, silver-black. Emmanuel laughs. Jonas laughs. Wally doesn't. He wipes the blade on his sleeve.

WALLY (CONT'D)
You walked far?

JONAS
All night.

WALLY
In that wind.

JONAS
It wasn't so bad.

Wally's gaze drops to Jonas's boots. They are clean. No snow packed in the treads, no ice at the laces. Wally says nothing.

EMMANUEL
Jonas, what do you do?

JONAS
I'm a surveyor.

EMMANUEL
For who?

JONAS
The county.

Emmanuel nods. Wally tilts his head and studies Jonas's face, the thin line of his smile.

WALLY
Emmanuel.

EMMANUEL
Hm?

WALLY
Come help me with the stove.

EMMANUEL
It's fine.

WALLY
Come help me.

Emmanuel stands. They cross to the stove together. Wally leans close, mouth by Emmanuel's ear.

WALLY (CONT'D)
(whispering)
He's got no breath.

EMMANUEL
What?

WALLY
In this cold. Watch.

Emmanuel looks. Jonas sits by the table, his face pale in the lamp. No fog drifts from his lips. Not one thread.

Jonas turns his head and smiles, wide. The stove light catches his eyes, and for a moment they are the colour of lake water under ice.

JONAS
Don't stop on my account.

Wally lifts the knife. Emmanuel grabs the poker. Jonas stands up slowly, unfolding to a height the shanty cannot hold. His head brushes the ceiling.

JONAS (CONT'D)
(softly)
I was only looking for company.

The lines in the holes go taut at once, all together, and something far below begins to pull.
